title:: BMVirtualController
summary:: Documentation BEASTmulch - generic software based controller
categories:: Classes

Description::

BMVirtualController is a generic software based controller. It provides a back end model and could be represented by any number of GUIs. One example, link::Classes/Controllers/BMVirtualControllerSliders::, is provided in the library. 

See also: link::Classes/Root Classes/BMAbstractController::, link::Classes/Controllers/BMVirtualControllerSliders::

Section:: Automation

This class is automatable, but when creating GUI classes to represent the state of this controller, it will likely be necessary to separate GUI updates from automation updates, since too high a GUI refresh rate will cause the system to hang (spinning beachball). BMVirtualControllerSliders provides one example of how to do this using a refresh loop which is stopped and started as necessary.

CLASSMETHODS::

method:: new
Create a new BMVirtualController.

argument:: name
A Symbol indicating the name of this controller.
argument:: server
An instance of Server. If you pass nil this will be the default Server.
argument:: numControls
An Integer indicating the number of controls this controller will have. The default value is 8.
		
code::
c = BMVirtualController.new(\sliders, s, 4); // new controller
g = BMVirtualControllerSliders(c);

g.close; c.free; // free resources
::

method:: newFromParamDict
	
Create a new instance using a parameter dictionary. (See link::Classes/Root Classes/BMAbstractController:: for more information.)

argument:: dict
A Dictionary or subclass with values for the following keys: code::\numControls:: -> An Integer indicating the number of controls, code::\name:: -> A Symbol indicating the name of this controller.
argument:: server
An instance of Server. If you pass nil this will be the default Server.


method:: parameterList
See link::Classes/Root Classes/BMAbstractController:: for a discussion of this method. This is the implementation for BMVirtualController:
		
code::
*parameterList { 
	var class;
	class = this;
	^(
		name: [Symbol, {class.makeName}, "Name"],
		numControls: [Integer, [1, 64, \linear, 1].asSpec, "Number of Faders"]
	); 
}
::

method:: humanName
Returns the String "GUI Controller".


